#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# coulomb_g

#% text
# `coulomb_g(l, eta, rho)` gives the irregular Coulomb wave function `G_l(eta, rho)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `G_l = H+_l - i F_l` with `H+_l` as in `coulomb_h1` (DLMF 33.2.7-33.2.8).

#% item
# At `eta = 0`, `G_l(0, rho) = -rho y_l(rho)`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values; integer and half-integer `l` (an integer `2 l + 2`) use the integer branch of `hypergeometric_u`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
coulomb_g(3, 0.12, 7.0)
#% output
#= -1.0693069229244365

#% cell
n(coulomb_g(3, 12/100, 3), 30)
#% output
#= 1.625113876479269664022347040484569

#% cell
coulomb_g(l, 0, r)
#% output
#= -r*spherical_bessel_y(l, r)

#% text
# The Wronskian `F' G - F G' = 1`, by difference quotients:

#% cell
h = 1.0e-5; r0 = 2.5; (coulomb_f(1, 0.4, r0 + h) - coulomb_f(1, 0.4, r0 - h))/(2*h)*coulomb_g(1, 0.4, r0) - coulomb_f(1, 0.4, r0)*(coulomb_g(1, 0.4, r0 + h) - coulomb_g(1, 0.4, r0 - h))/(2*h)
#% output
#= 0.9999999999982196

